UNIT parse_wire
LANG rust
PRIMARY-SHA256 4c4e81554252a1204b31465947429f7ed248b5f7d92b440fde8d9c576c9ef1a6
SIG pub fn parse_wire(line: &str) -> Result<serde_json::Value, String>
ABI crate shape: the artifact is a LIBRARY crate (`[lib]`, `src/lib.rs` exporting `pub fn parse_wire`) — no main, no I/O,
  no binary. The verifier generates its own harness crate from the abstract SIG: one JSON array of args per stdin line, the
  line bound as &str, then one {"ok": value} or {"throw": message} line. Compiling that harness against this SIG is the
  signature check.
ABI type mapping: json = serde_json::Value: none = Null; bool = Bool; text = String; list = Array; map = Object (last
  duplicate kept, R4); f64 = Number. Ok(value) for a result; Err(message) for a throw (R1, R5, R8; the message is not
  graded). The harness prints the Value and the verifier reads it back through the package's own parseWire, so a Number
  keeps what it is: an integer's digits cross unchanged at any width (arbitrary_precision, MECHANISM), an f64 crosses as its
  shortest round-trip form, and -0.0 is written "-0.0" and read back as negative zero (R5, R6).
ENVELOPE narrowing: the vectors whose result holds an R6 exact integer or a lone surrogate are not in the shared oracle (the
  neutral codec has no form for either; js and ts grade them) — yet the R6 integers are graded here too, by cross-verification
  against js and ts on the same lines, so they must come back exact. OUT-OF-DOMAIN is js only.
MECHANISM the reader is HAND-WRITTEN: recursive descent over the line, R1-R8 as the primary states them, building a
  serde_json::Value. serde_json::from_str is NOT this unit, with or without features (checked on serde_json 1.0.154 against the
  pinned parseWire): by default an integer past u64/i64 is rounded to the nearest f64 ("184467440573709551616" comes back
  184467440573709550000, R6 forbids it) and one past the double range is Err (R6 never throws: a 401-digit integer overwritten by a
  repeated key, '{"a":1000...0,"a":1}', must read as {a:1}); with arbitrary_precision it accepts "1e400" (R5 throws); and in every mode it rejects an unpaired
  surrogate escape, which R3 reads ('{"a":"\ud800","a":1}' must read as {a:1}) EARNED round-1 cross 184467440573709551616
MECHANISM numbers, by class (REQUIRED feature arbitrary_precision: without it serde_json::Number cannot hold an integer past
  64 bits, and nothing else in the SIG can). The harness prints the returned Value and the verifier reads it back through the
  package's own parseWire, which applies R5 and R6 to the printed digits — so the artifact's job is to hand over the RIGHT
  DIGITS. An INTEGER-SPELLING numeral, of ANY width and either sign, "-0" included: Value::Number made from its source text
  verbatim (text.parse::<serde_json::Number>(), which under arbitrary_precision keeps the digits) — never via i64, u64 or f64
  first (i64 drops the sign of "-0"; f64 rounds 2^53 + 1, 2^64 + 1 and every wider integer; an overflowing parse turns
  10^400 into an error). A numeral with a fraction or an exponent: str::parse::<f64>() (correctly rounded, ties to even, sign
  of zero kept — R5); a non-finite result -> Err (R5); else Value::Number(Number::from_f64(x)), which prints the shortest form
  that reads back as x ("-0.0" for negative zero) EARNED round-1 cross 115292150460684697636893488147419103232
MECHANISM text: a Rust String holds no lone surrogate, so collect each string's UTF-16 code units (an escape's XXXX, and each
  literal character's own units) and convert with String::from_utf16_lossy — an unpaired unit becomes U+FFFD and the read
  CONTINUES, never Err (those results are js/ts only; an overwritten one must still read). Keys go through the same reader.
  Maps: serde_json::Map, insert in order (a repeated key keeps its last value, R4), every value read first. Depth: R8's 128
  checked on entry to each value. Err(message) for every throw; the message is not graded.
DEPENDS-ON serde_json (crate, version 1, features = ["arbitrary_precision", "float_roundtrip"]) — the SIG's value type; the
  reader itself is the artifact's own code. float_roundtrip matches the verifier's harness (Cargo unifies features), and
  arbitrary_precision reaches the harness the same way, which prints the integer digits unchanged
PROVENANCE rederive@f68cca701417 cli/lang/rust.mjs parseWire (byte-identical to 1d1861440f2c and 6199e2cc8b75). HARDEN round 1 (cross
  1995/1998 on R6 integers past 64 bits): the serde_json claims were re-checked against serde_json 1.0.154 in three builds
  (default + float_roundtrip, + arbitrary_precision, and the hand-written reader above) on 40,000 seeded lines; only the
  hand-written reader agrees with parseWire on every line inside this ENVELOPE.
